===Etymology===
Mid-17th century. Equivalent to {{com|en|name|sake}}. From the phrase "for (one's) [[name]]'s [[sake]]", first found in Bible translations as a rendering of a Hebrew idiom meaning "to protect one's reputation" or possibly "vouched for by one's reputation." A familiar example is in Psalm 23:3, "he leadeth me in the paths of righteousness for his name's sake" (King James Bible, 1604).

